= Reference =

== Overview ==
'''Reference''' is the relation or act by which an expression or representation is used to pick out, concern, or stand for something.

In language, names, pronouns, demonstratives, and descriptions can be used referentially. Different theories disagree about how reference is established and about how reference relates to meaning.

== Reference and meaning ==
Reference is not the whole of [[Semantics|meaning]].

Two expressions can refer to the same entity while differing in informational role or sense. Conversely, an expression can be meaningful even when it lacks a successful real-world referent.

Therefore:

<pre>
reference ≠ meaning
co-reference ≠ synonymy
</pre>

== Reference and identity ==
[[Identity]] is strict sameness: whether two descriptions concern one and the same thing.

Reference is the representational relation by which a term or act concerns a target.

Two expressions may co-refer, but evidence that they do so is not itself the identity relation. An identifier or label also does not guarantee successful reference.

== Referring expressions ==
Common cases include:

* proper names;
* personal pronouns;
* demonstratives such as “this” and “that”;
* indexicals such as “I”, “here”, and “now”;
* definite descriptions;
* some quantified or anaphoric expressions.

Their interpretation can depend on [[Context (linguistics)|context]], linguistic structure, discourse, and speaker intentions.

== Major theories ==
Philosophy of language and formal semantics include descriptive, causal-historical, direct-reference, sense-based, and pragmatic approaches.

Classic debates concern whether names refer through descriptions, causal chains, conventions, or direct semantic rules.

No single historical theory should be treated as the definition of reference itself.

== Reference in formal systems ==
Formal semantics often distinguishes syntactic expressions from their denotations under an interpretation.

For an interpretation I, an expression e may receive a semantic value:

<pre>
⟦e⟧ᵢ
</pre>

This mathematical relation is precisely defined within the model. It does not by itself prove what a real-world community intends an identifier to denote.

== Reference failure ==
Expressions can fail to refer, refer ambiguously, or be used fictionally, hypothetically, generically, or incorrectly.

Theories differ over the semantic consequences of failed reference.

